<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hu Shih</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hu Shih was a Chinese academic, writer, diplomat and politician who contributed to Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and was a leading advocate of written vernacular Chinese. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, he studied at Cornell and at Columbia under John Dewey, whose translator he became, then lectured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a> and became one of the leading intellectuals of the May Fourth and New Culture Movements. He promoted vernacular Chinese in literature in place of Classical Chinese. He was the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s ambassador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1938 to 1942 and, from 1957 until his death, president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academia-sinica">Academia Sinica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a>; in the 1950s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> campaigned against his thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hu-shih.jpg" alt="Hu Shih" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hu_Shih_1960_color_Crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In literature <b>Hu Shih</b>, who called the era the “Chinese Renaissance”, had argued for the modern vernacular in his 1917 essay “Preliminary discussion on literary reform”, and Chen Hengzhe published the short story One Day in 1917, a year before Lu Xun’s Diary of a Madman.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The scholar <b>Hu Shih</b> called Han Fei and Li Si the “greatest statesmen in Chinese history” and champions of progress through conscious human effort, and Feng Youlan read the Legalists as statesmen who understood that needs change with the times.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
